# The vault must actually be zero-knowledge (FR-5.1, FR-5.2, plan §3.2)

A 4-digit PIN has **10,000 possible values**. Against an attacker holding the database, no key
derivation function saves you — an expensive KDF turns an instant break into a few hours of GPU
time. The specification's original PBKDF2-from-PIN design does not deliver the property it promises.

## Split the key

```
wrapping_key = Argon2id(
    password = user_pin || server_secret,
    salt     = per_note_salt,
    m = 64 MiB, t = 3, p = 1
)
```

`server_secret` lives in a **separate keystore, never in the application database**, and is released
only to an authenticated, rate-limited, lockout-protected session. A stolen dump then contains no
brute-forceable material, because the attacker holds only half the input. The therapist still types
four digits; their experience is unchanged.

## Non-negotiable

- **Argon2id**, not PBKDF2. OWASP has recommended it since 2021.
- **No administrator override, no recovery path, no server-side key capable of decryption alone.**
  If you can write a code path that reveals a note without the patient's PIN, FR-5.2 is broken. This
  is a gate (§10.1), asserted against a full dump plus every server-held secret.
- Keys live in **JavaScript closure memory only**. Never `localStorage`, never `sessionStorage`,
  never a cookie, never application state that a devtools inspection walks.
- Auto re-lock after **10 minutes idle**; locked is the default state.
- Every unlock writes to the append-only audit log.
- When locked, only demographic and payment metadata is visible. All clinical text is masked.

`prototype/PractixOS.html` uses PBKDF2 because the Web Crypto API offers no Argon2id, and says so on
screen. That is a prototype limitation. Do not carry it into production.
